1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,580
December 1812. A handful of frozen,

2
00:00:03,810 --> 00:00:06,150
starving men stumble across a bridge over

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:08,330
the Niemen River, at the western edge of

4
00:00:08,330 --> 00:00:09,320
the Russian Empire.

5
00:00:10,014 --> 00:00:13,634
Six months earlier, more than 450,000 soldiers

6
00:00:13,634 --> 00:00:15,684
had marched across this same river.

7
00:00:16,294 --> 00:00:18,254
One of the largest armies Europe had ever

8
00:00:18,254 --> 00:00:18,724
seen.

9
00:00:19,311 --> 00:00:21,161
Most of them never came back.

10
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:23,690
History blames the Russian winter.

11
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:26,320
But the army was already half gone before

12
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:27,530
the first snow fell.

13
00:00:28,197 --> 00:00:30,317
This is the story of the mistake that cost

14
00:00:30,317 --> 00:00:32,347
Napoleon Bonaparte his empire.

15
00:00:33,701 --> 00:00:36,281
By 1812, Napoleon was the most powerful

16
00:00:36,281 --> 00:00:39,031
man in Europe. In a decade of war,

17
00:00:39,241 --> 00:00:40,351
he had crushed Austria,

18
00:00:40,621 --> 00:00:42,821
Prussia and Russia, and placed his own brothers

19
00:00:42,821 --> 00:00:44,771
on thrones across the continent.

20
00:00:45,465 --> 00:00:48,135
Only one enemy refused to bow: Britain.

21
00:00:48,685 --> 00:00:50,135
Protected by the Royal Navy,

22
00:00:50,465 --> 00:00:52,055
it was beyond the reach of his armies.

23
00:00:52,708 --> 00:00:54,988
So Napoleon tried to strangle Britain with

24
00:00:54,988 --> 00:00:57,678
trade. Under his Continental System,

25
00:00:58,008 --> 00:00:59,728
the ports of Europe were closed to British

26
00:00:59,728 --> 00:01:00,238
goods.

27
00:01:00,869 --> 00:01:03,379
Russia had joined the blockade in 1807.

28
00:01:03,949 --> 00:01:06,039
But it was crippling the Russian economy.

29
00:01:06,629 --> 00:01:08,359
And on the last day of 1810,

30
00:01:08,589 --> 00:01:10,799
Tsar Alexander I broke away.

31
00:01:11,367 --> 00:01:12,977
To Napoleon, it was betrayal.

32
00:01:13,647 --> 00:01:15,017
If Russia could defy him,

33
00:01:15,067 --> 00:01:17,367
others would follow. He decided to teach

34
00:01:17,367 --> 00:01:18,517
the Tsar a lesson.

35
00:01:19,245 --> 00:01:21,995
His plan was simple: march into Russia,

36
00:01:22,145 --> 00:01:24,245
destroy the Russian army in one decisive

37
00:01:24,245 --> 00:01:26,765
battle within weeks, and force Alexander

38
00:01:26,765 --> 00:01:28,095
back into line.

39
00:01:28,574 --> 00:01:30,754
He assembled a force unlike anything seen

40
00:01:30,754 --> 00:01:34,074
before. More than 450,000 men.

41
00:01:34,074 --> 00:01:38,564
150,000 horses. 25,000 wagons.

42
00:01:38,814 --> 00:01:40,484
And nearly 1,400 cannon.

43
00:01:41,070 --> 00:01:42,740
Only about half of them were French.

44
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:46,030
Poles, Germans, Italians, Austrians and

45
00:01:46,030 --> 00:01:47,780
Swiss marched under the same eagle.

46
00:01:48,281 --> 00:01:50,351
On June 24, 1812,

47
00:01:50,781 --> 00:01:52,721
the Grande Armée crossed the Niemen River

48
00:01:52,721 --> 00:01:53,411
into Russia.

49
00:01:54,476 --> 00:01:56,716
Napoleon expected the Russians to stand

50
00:01:56,716 --> 00:01:58,566
and fight. They did not.

51
00:01:59,136 --> 00:02:00,316
The Russian armies retreated,

52
00:02:00,316 --> 00:02:02,986
deeper and deeper into their vast country,

53
00:02:03,096 --> 00:02:05,576
burning crops, villages and supplies as

54
00:02:05,576 --> 00:02:06,126
they went.

55
00:02:06,665 --> 00:02:09,135
The Grande Armée marched into an empty land,

56
00:02:09,665 --> 00:02:11,795
and its supply wagons could not keep up.

57
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:13,380
The summer was brutal.

58
00:02:13,670 --> 00:02:15,280
Men collapsed from heat and thirst.

59
00:02:15,990 --> 00:02:17,690
Dysentery and typhus spread through the

60
00:02:17,690 --> 00:02:18,060
ranks.

61
00:02:18,687 --> 00:02:20,417
Horses died by the thousands.

62
00:02:21,107 --> 00:02:23,237
By the time Napoleon reached Vilnius,

63
00:02:23,507 --> 00:02:26,477
more than 5,000 dead horses lay behind him.

64
00:02:26,885 --> 00:02:28,355
In just the first eight weeks,

65
00:02:28,625 --> 00:02:30,785
the main army lost a third of its strength,

66
00:02:30,785 --> 00:02:33,635
before a single major battle had been fought.

67
00:02:34,211 --> 00:02:36,341
In August, the Russians fought at Smolensk,

68
00:02:36,711 --> 00:02:38,001
then slipped away again,

69
00:02:38,091 --> 00:02:39,341
leaving the city in flames.

70
00:02:40,173 --> 00:02:41,993
The Tsar now gave command to an old,

71
00:02:41,993 --> 00:02:45,387
battle-scarred general: Mikhail Kutuzov.

72
00:02:46,137 --> 00:02:49,177
On September 7, near the village of Borodino,

73
00:02:49,177 --> 00:02:50,997
about 75 miles from Moscow,

74
00:02:50,997 --> 00:02:53,647
Kutuzov finally stood his ground.

75
00:02:54,211 --> 00:02:56,211
About a quarter of a million soldiers clashed

76
00:02:56,211 --> 00:02:57,521
in a single day.

77
00:02:57,991 --> 00:03:00,291
It became the bloodiest day of the Napoleonic

78
00:03:00,291 --> 00:03:00,781
Wars.

79
00:03:01,406 --> 00:03:04,046
By nightfall, more than 70,000 men had been

80
00:03:04,046 --> 00:03:05,236
killed or wounded.

81
00:03:05,932 --> 00:03:07,422
Napoleon held the field.

82
00:03:07,812 --> 00:03:09,242
But at the decisive moment,

83
00:03:09,412 --> 00:03:11,412
he refused to send in his elite Imperial

84
00:03:11,412 --> 00:03:12,042
Guard.

85
00:03:12,604 --> 00:03:14,194
The Russian army pulled back,

86
00:03:14,264 --> 00:03:16,944
bloodied but alive. The decisive victory

87
00:03:16,944 --> 00:03:18,414
he needed had slipped away.

88
00:03:19,459 --> 00:03:21,839
A week later, on September 14,

89
00:03:21,839 --> 00:03:24,939
Napoleon rode into Moscow with roughly 100,000

90
00:03:24,939 --> 00:03:27,699
men. He expected nobles to greet him with

91
00:03:27,699 --> 00:03:28,769
the keys of the city.

92
00:03:29,279 --> 00:03:31,969
No one came. Moscow was almost empty.

93
00:03:32,731 --> 00:03:35,061
That night, fires broke out across the city.

94
00:03:35,671 --> 00:03:37,851
Within days, three-quarters of Moscow had

95
00:03:37,851 --> 00:03:39,741
burned. The city's governor,

96
00:03:39,891 --> 00:03:42,411
Count Rostopchin, is widely believed to

97
00:03:42,411 --> 00:03:43,761
have organized the destruction.

98
00:03:44,945 --> 00:03:47,045
And here, Napoleon made the decision that

99
00:03:47,045 --> 00:03:48,915
sealed his fate. He waited.

100
00:03:49,816 --> 00:03:52,086
Convinced the Tsar would now ask for peace,

101
00:03:52,616 --> 00:03:54,876
he stayed in the ruins of Moscow for five

102
00:03:54,876 --> 00:03:56,886
weeks, sending letters to Alexander.

103
00:03:57,670 --> 00:03:59,580
Alexander refused to answer.

104
00:04:00,313 --> 00:04:01,833
Every day Napoleon waited,

105
00:04:01,833 --> 00:04:04,003
Kutuzov's army grew stronger,

106
00:04:04,533 --> 00:04:06,083
and the Russian winter came closer.

107
00:04:06,755 --> 00:04:07,865
This was the mistake.

108
00:04:08,855 --> 00:04:10,675
Napoleon believed that capturing a capital

109
00:04:10,675 --> 00:04:11,595
would end the war,

110
00:04:11,595 --> 00:04:13,065
as it always had before.

111
00:04:13,555 --> 00:04:15,145
But Russia was not Austria.

112
00:04:15,535 --> 00:04:17,505
And Alexander was not going to surrender.

113
00:04:18,281 --> 00:04:21,431
On October 19, Napoleon finally left Moscow.

114
00:04:22,001 --> 00:04:23,571
He had waited 35 days.

115
00:04:24,685 --> 00:04:26,445
He tried to take a southern road through

116
00:04:26,445 --> 00:04:29,505
untouched country. But at Maloyaroslavets,

117
00:04:29,505 --> 00:04:30,835
the Russians blocked the way.

118
00:04:31,225 --> 00:04:33,465
The army was forced back onto the same road

119
00:04:33,465 --> 00:04:34,415
it had come by,

120
00:04:34,745 --> 00:04:36,415
a road it had already stripped bare.

121
00:04:37,021 --> 00:04:38,521
Then, on November 6,

122
00:04:38,521 --> 00:04:39,371
the winter arrived.

123
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:42,270
The soldiers were still wearing summer uniforms.

124
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:44,730
The horses had no winter shoes.

125
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:46,420
They slipped on the icy roads,

126
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:47,150
and died.

127
00:04:47,692 --> 00:04:50,172
Without horses, the cannon and supply wagons

128
00:04:50,172 --> 00:04:52,772
were abandoned. Men fought over horse meat,

129
00:04:52,772 --> 00:04:54,202
and froze where they slept.

130
00:04:54,776 --> 00:04:56,756
Cossacks and Russian peasants struck the

131
00:04:56,756 --> 00:04:58,466
stragglers, day and night.

132
00:04:58,852 --> 00:05:00,972
At night, temperatures plunged far below

133
00:05:00,972 --> 00:05:03,992
zero, at times to minus thirty degrees and

134
00:05:03,992 --> 00:05:04,422
colder.

135
00:05:05,509 --> 00:05:07,909
In late November, the army reached the Berezina

136
00:05:07,909 --> 00:05:10,409
River, with Russian armies closing in from

137
00:05:10,409 --> 00:05:12,939
three sides. The bridges were gone.

138
00:05:13,438 --> 00:05:15,698
Napoleon's engineers waded into the freezing

139
00:05:15,698 --> 00:05:17,428
water to build two new ones.

140
00:05:18,038 --> 00:05:20,058
Their commander, General Eblé,

141
00:05:20,058 --> 00:05:22,638
had disobeyed orders to destroy his bridging

142
00:05:22,638 --> 00:05:25,478
tools. That disobedience saved what was

143
00:05:25,478 --> 00:05:26,408
left of the army.

144
00:05:26,915 --> 00:05:29,195
For three days, the army crossed under Russian

145
00:05:29,195 --> 00:05:32,075
fire. Tens of thousands never made it to

146
00:05:32,075 --> 00:05:32,845
the other side.

147
00:05:33,564 --> 00:05:36,464
On December 5, Napoleon left his army behind

148
00:05:36,464 --> 00:05:38,874
and raced back to Paris by sleigh.

149
00:05:39,404 --> 00:05:41,674
Marshal Michel Ney commanded the rear guard.

150
00:05:42,224 --> 00:05:43,604
He is said to have been the last French

151
00:05:43,604 --> 00:05:45,514
soldier to leave Russian soil.

152
00:05:46,665 --> 00:05:49,785
Of the 450,000 men who had entered Russia

153
00:05:49,785 --> 00:05:50,915
with the main army,

154
00:05:51,365 --> 00:05:53,335
only a small fraction came back.

155
00:05:53,885 --> 00:05:55,685
Estimates of the French and allied dead

156
00:05:55,685 --> 00:05:57,715
run to 300,000 or more.

157
00:05:58,158 --> 00:06:00,578
Decades later, a French engineer named Charles

158
00:06:00,578 --> 00:06:03,158
Joseph Minard drew the disaster as a single

159
00:06:03,158 --> 00:06:07,518
graphic. The army shrinks from 422,000 men

160
00:06:07,518 --> 00:06:09,330
to 10,000.

161
00:06:09,580 --> 00:06:11,200
It has been called the best statistical

162
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:12,650
graphic ever drawn.

163
00:06:13,522 --> 00:06:16,192
Napoleon's aura of invincibility was gone.

164
00:06:16,842 --> 00:06:18,852
Prussia and Austria turned against him,

165
00:06:19,062 --> 00:06:21,642
and in 1813, the armies of Europe crushed

166
00:06:21,642 --> 00:06:22,672
him at Leipzig.

167
00:06:23,122 --> 00:06:26,482
In April 1814, Napoleon gave up his throne

168
00:06:26,482 --> 00:06:29,092
and was exiled to the island of Elba.

169
00:06:29,492 --> 00:06:31,072
He would return one last time,

170
00:06:31,072 --> 00:06:32,512
for a hundred days,

171
00:06:32,512 --> 00:06:34,742
and meet his final defeat at Waterloo.

172
00:06:35,818 --> 00:06:38,028
So what really destroyed the Grande Armée?

173
00:06:38,358 --> 00:06:40,068
Not one battle. Not even the winter.

174
00:06:40,766 --> 00:06:42,566
It was a gamble: that one battle,

175
00:06:42,566 --> 00:06:43,846
or one captured city,

176
00:06:43,846 --> 00:06:45,996
would force an empire to surrender.

177
00:06:46,746 --> 00:06:49,126
And 35 days of waiting in a burning city,

178
00:06:49,126 --> 00:06:51,096
while the winter marched toward him.

179
00:06:51,811 --> 00:06:53,711
Was it Napoleon's greatest mistake,

180
00:06:53,711 --> 00:06:56,341
or was Russia simply unconquerable?

181
00:06:56,951 --> 00:06:57,751
Tell us in the comments,

182
00:06:57,751 --> 00:07:00,431
and subscribe to Buried Chronicles for more

183
00:07:00,431 --> 00:07:01,761
of history's turning points.

